  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nSatellite imagery hints at a subtle shortwave beginning to roll \nthrough the Lake Superior early this morning, allowing lake enhanced \nrain showers to develop over mainly the eastern half of the U.P. by \nthe late morning hours. That being said, some sprinkles could be see \nfor a few hours over the Keweenaw and north central even as high \npressure starts to build back into the region by the afternoon \nhours. With PWATs around 0.50 to 0.75 inches today and delta-Ts \ngetting into the upper teens, we could see some spots in the east \nget a tenth of an inch or two before the rain ceases late this \nafternoon. However, with the gusty winds being WNW today, expect \nmost of the lake enhanced rain showers to fall east of Munising and \nnorth of M-28. The gusty winds will create dangerous swimming\nconditions along Alger County and potentially even in Marquette\nCounty near the Alger County border (i.e. the Shot Point \narea); thus a Beach Hazard Statement has been issued for Alger\nCounty today. Elsewhere across Upper Michigan today, expect \npretty dry weather as temperatures will be cooler than yesterday\n(highs in the 60s, mostly the lower half). As high pressure \nbuilds in tonight, expect the clear skies and antecedent cooler \nair aloft to allow temperatures to drop into the 30s across much\nof the interior area, with the interior west looking to dip \ninto the 20s. This will allow for frost to develop over the \ninterior west tonight, potentially damaging unprotected plants \nand crops. Around normal temperatures with highs in the upper \n60s returns to the area on Monday as a warm front approaches the\nU.P. from the southwest. \n\nOur next real rain chance comes with the warm front that occludes \nover us late Monday through Monday night. While strong atmospheric \nshear, helicity, and a LLJ are lining up over the area late Monday \ninto Monday night, with CAPE being about 0 J/kg, no severe weather \nis expected over the U.P. with this system; the threat is further \nsouth in Wisconsin. That being said, while the energy within the \natmosphere is lacking for severe convection, with PWATs reaching up \nto 2.00 inches in spots (highest along the WI border) and with \nabundant synoptic-scale forcing available, some heavy rainfall could \nbe seen across the area Monday evening to Tuesday morning; the mean \nNBM has generally a soaking (1 inch) rainfall event across the \nentire U.P. late Monday through Tuesday morning, with the \nprobabilities for an inch or more of rainfall at any spot in the \nU.P. ranging from 40% to 70%. Hopefully, this system rainfall will \nlead to drought improvement across the area as severe to even \nextreme drought in the far west holds across portions of the U.P. \nstill. Outside of the rainfall, expect windy conditions with the \noccluding front's passing and behind it; expect to see southerly \nwinds of 25 to 35 mph Monday night with the occluding front followed \nby westerly wind gusts of 40 to 50 mph along the Lake Superior \nshoreline behind it on Tuesday (a Wind Advisory may be needed for \nthe Keweenaw Peninsula in the future if the forecast holds). \n\nExpect flattening ridging to move back into the area for the mid-\nweek as near normal temperatures and dry conditions dominate. We \ncould see some more patchy frost over the interior west on Tuesday \nnight, but uncertainty on that is still high at this time given that \nit's 3 days out and the incoming rainfall late Monday. We may also \nsee rainfall one more time late next week, but given the high \npressure ridge projected over the Southeastern U.S., there are some \ndoubts on if any shortwave activity will move over the U.P. or not. \nRegardless, expect near normal temperatures to continue through the \nend of the week.",
